body:
Emitting an anchor whose name contains `~`, and an alias to it, puts the emitter in an error state (INVALID_ANCHOR), although `~` is a valid character in a YAML anchor name and the parser accepts such names.

Repro:
```cpp
YAML::Emitter out;
out << YAML::BeginSeq;
out << YAML::Anchor("foo~bar") << "value";
out << YAML::Alias("foo~bar");
out << YAML::EndSeq;
```
Expected output: `- &foo~bar value\n- *foo~bar`.

Tags set programmatically on a node are emitted wrongly in two cases.

```cpp
YAML::Node node, root;
node = 42;
node.SetTag("hello");
root["num"] = node;
YAML::Emitter e; e << root;
```
Expected `num: !<hello> 42`.

With `node.SetTag("!a!foo")` the expected output is `num: !a!foo 42`.

```cpp
YAML::Emitter out;
out << YAML::BeginSeq;
out << std::uint8_t{16};
out << YAML::EndSeq;
```
produces a control character instead of `- 16`. Eight-bit unsigned integers should be emitted as numbers like the other integer types.
